Game Idea

The game is centred around a society/empire where the player assumes the role of the leader of the people and their leadership skill is tested. The setting cuts across historical years and every level is represented by a year in history. 1st order rule – The player makes decisions which earn them resources – points – but then they are also expected to make a major decision which triggers a consequence that they cannot reverse. They can only make further decisions to adapt to the society their choice has created in the game but not go back to change it There is no winner in the game because the player/leader does not win; the game ends only with a society that their choices built. The game intends to teach people how to live with the consequences of their own actions, especially in the face of constant factors that they do not have the power to change, and will be approached from a historical perspective, giving digital voice to those history often notes as the colonised.
Game Identity / Mantra: 
A yearly strategy game about governing a society through consequences players chose but can never take back. 
Design Pillars:
Weighty. Irrevocable. Humbling.
Weighty: every decision should feel like it has mass, not just game-play consequences, but connected emotionally or morally
Irrevocable: the game is fundamentally about living in the magic circle with the tension that comes from knowing there's no undo to one’s action and the player needs to live with it 
Humbling: the fact that there is no winner and the player only feels the scale of what their actions have caused or cannot control (said cannot control because to some extent the options/mechanism they have within the game was set by me and that they can control to some extent as they have options to choose from), which also results in a 

Game description: 

A societal strategy game where only the smartest minds govern, the hasty sacrifice and endure. This game will test how smart a leader you are. 

Mechanics Summary:
Make decisions: The player can make decisions, i.e., purchase resources, sign treaties, and dialogue with neighbouring villages or towns which they are able to adjust or go back on. But then they have to make a single irreversible reckoning choice per year to tell a historical story about leadership and their people who live in the era have to live inside the consequences of decisions they never got to make but which their ruler made for them. The player understands the weight of their reckoning choice may bring upon them  and the subsequent parts of the game reflect on their decision.

The players can collect resources;  they can trade and acquire wealth, which they can use to try to resolve the societal problem that arises from their ruler's decision. I.e they can use the resources accumulated to ward off colonial masters or neighbouring invaders, buy potions for diseases, and pay tributes when they commit an offence, i.e., for leading a protest 

The players can react to the rule of their leader by protesting , advising... Thinking of adding some advisers among the populace

Loop 

First loop: Govern/Rule 

Second loop: Make a minor/series of minor decisions – which either cost the village/town something or bring them something favourable.

Third loop: The irreversible decision is made.

Fourth loop: The ruler tries to salvage his action/adapt 

Fifth loop: The people protest/revolt/rejoice depending on the consequences of the ruler's irreversible choice 

Sixth loop: The society created is shown


